    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>When we first published this article in July 2023, the big side hustle questions were about the basics: what is a side hustle, is it worth the time, and what are the pros and cons. Those questions still matter, but the landscape around them has shifted. AI tools went mainstream, platforms rewrote their fee structures, gig apps changed how they pay, and Congress reversed a tax reporting rule that had worried millions of sellers. This update covers what a side hustle is today, what changed since 2023, and where things are headed.</p>

<div class="stat-row">
  <div class="stat"><b>$20,000</b><span>1099-K threshold (and 200+ transactions)</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><b>$2,000</b><span>new 1099-NEC threshold for 2026</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><b>15.3%</b><span>self-employment tax, unchanged</span></div>
</div>

<h2 id="what-is-a-side-hustle">What is a side hustle?</h2>
<p>A side hustle is income you earn outside your primary job, usually with flexible hours and some control over what you charge. The common types:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Gig work:</strong> delivery, rideshare, errands and pet care through apps like DoorDash, <a href="/lyft-uber/">Uber and Lyft</a>, Instacart and Rover.</li>
  <li><strong>Freelancing:</strong> selling a skill such as writing, design, marketing or bookkeeping, directly or through platforms like <a href="/fiverr/">Fiverr</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Selling products:</strong> reselling, handmade goods, print-on-demand or a small online store on <a href="/shopify/">Shopify</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Digital products and teaching:</strong> templates, ebooks and online courses on platforms like <a href="/thinkific/">Thinkific</a> or Udemy.</li>
  <li><strong>Local services:</strong> cleaning, lawn care, pressure washing, handyman work and tutoring.</li>
</ul>
<p>What separates a side hustle from a second job is control. You decide when you work and, in most cases, what you charge. That flexibility is also why the income is less predictable.</p>

<h3 id="side-hustle-vs-second-job">Side hustle vs. second job vs. small business</h3>
<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Side hustle</th><th>Second job</th><th>Small business</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who sets hours</td><td>You</td><td>Employer</td><td>You (but customers drive demand)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay</td><td>Variable; per task, project or sale</td><td>Fixed hourly wage</td><td>Profit after expenses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxes</td><td>Self-employment tax on net profit</td><td>Withheld from paycheck</td><td>Self-employment or business taxes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Benefits</td><td>None</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Only what you buy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upside</td><td>Medium; can grow into a business</td><td>Capped by hours</td><td>Highest, with the most risk</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Plenty of small businesses started as side hustles. The difference is mostly scale and intent: once you are hiring help, registering a business entity and reinvesting profits, you have crossed over.</p>

<h2 id="pros-and-cons">Pros and cons, revisited</h2>
<p>The trade-offs we listed in 2023 have held up, with a few updates:</p>
<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Advantage</th><th>2026 reality</th><th>Disadvantage</th><th>2026 reality</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Extra income</td><td>Still the top reason; helps with higher living costs</td><td>Less free time</td><td>Unchanged; hour limits matter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Better: more instant payouts and on-demand apps</td><td>Irregular income</td><td>Still true; algorithms change pay without notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skill building</td><td>Stronger: AI tools speed up learning</td><td>Self-employment tax</td><td>Still 15.3% on net earnings of $400+</td></tr>
<tr><td>Path to self-employment</td><td>Easier with cheap tools and platforms</td><td>More competition</td><td>Low-skill online tasks pay less than before</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="what-changed">What changed since 2023</h2>

<h3 id="ai-tools">1. AI became part of almost every online side hustle</h3>
<p>In 2023, AI writing tools were new and controversial. By 2026 they are simply part of the toolkit. Freelancers use them to draft, research, summarize, edit video and generate design variations. The effect cuts both ways: one person can now deliver far more, but clients expect faster turnaround, and generic content work (basic blog posts, simple product descriptions, stock-style images) pays noticeably less. The freelancers doing best sell judgment and results, not volume.</p>

<h3 id="ai-gigs">2. A new category: AI-assisted side hustles</h3>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="ai-training">AI training and evaluation</h3>
    <p>Companies pay people to rate, compare and correct AI responses. Generalist work pays modestly; specialists in coding, math, law, medicine or a second language earn the most. Work volume can swing week to week.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Roughly $15–$50/hr by skill</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Laptop, assessments</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Experts and careful writers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="ai-automation">Automations for small businesses</h3>
    <p>Setting up chatbots, booking flows, email automations and AI-powered workflows for local businesses that don't have time to figure it out.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $300–$2,000 per project</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$50/mo tools</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Tech-comfortable people</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="ai-freelancing">AI-assisted freelancing</h3>
    <p>Writing, design, video editing and marketing delivered faster with AI, then refined by you. The edge is quality control and knowing the client's industry.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$100+/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$30/mo</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Existing skill holders</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> "Make $5,000 a month with AI on autopilot" courses were everywhere in 2023 and still are. Mass-produced AI ebooks, faceless channels and spam blogs rarely earn anything, and platforms increasingly filter them out.</div>

<h3 id="platform-fees">3. Platform fees shifted</h3>
<p>Fees decide how much of your gross income you keep, and several big platforms changed theirs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Upwork</strong> moved freelancers to a flat 10% fee in May 2023, then in 2025 switched to a variable fee of 0–15% per contract, shown before you submit a proposal.</li>
  <li><strong>Fiverr</strong> still takes 20% from sellers, while buyers pay a separate service fee on top.</li>
  <li><strong>Mercari</strong> has reworked its seller and buyer fees more than once since 2023, so check the current fee page before pricing.</li>
  <li><strong>TikTok Shop</strong> launched broadly in the US in fall 2023 and has raised its seller fees as it matured.</li>
  <li><strong>Etsy</strong> has kept its 6.5% transaction fee and $0.20 listing fee, plus payment processing, and Offsite Ads become mandatory once a shop passes $10,000 in yearly sales.</li>
</ul>
<p>The lesson: build your pricing with fees in mind, and avoid depending on a single platform. Use the <a href="/tools/freelance-rate-calculator/">freelance rate calculator</a> to set rates that survive platform cuts.</p>

<h3 id="gig-pay">4. Gig apps changed how they pay</h3>
<p>Instant and same-day cashouts became standard across DoorDash, Uber, Lyft and Instacart. Lyft introduced a guarantee in 2024 that drivers' weekly earnings, after external fees, will be at least 70% of what riders pay. Upfront pricing, where you see the trip or order payout before accepting, is now the norm, which makes it easier to decline unprofitable work. What has not changed: your real hourly rate depends on miles, gas and wait time, so check it with the <a href="/tools/gig-earnings-calculator/">gig earnings calculator</a>.</p>

<h3 id="tax-changes">5. Tax reporting rules flip-flopped, then settled</h3>
<p>This is the change that confused the most side hustlers. In 2023, payment apps like PayPal and Venmo and marketplaces like eBay and Etsy were set to send a Form 1099-K once you received just $600. The IRS delayed that repeatedly and phased in transitional thresholds of $5,000 for 2024 and a planned $2,500 for 2025.</p>
<p>Then the One Big Beautiful Bill Act of 2025 reversed course. The IRS confirmed in October 2025 that the law retroactively restored the original rule: platforms only need to issue a 1099-K when you receive <strong>more than $20,000 and more than 200 transactions</strong> in a year. The law also raised the threshold for Form 1099-NEC (what clients send freelancers) from $600 to $2,000 for payments made in 2026, with inflation adjustments after that.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong> Fewer forms does not mean less tax. All side hustle profit is taxable, and self-employment tax of 15.3% applies once you net $400 or more in a year. The 2025 law also created a temporary deduction for qualified tips, which may help some tipped gig workers, so ask a tax professional whether it applies to you.</div>
<p>Use the estimator below to see roughly how much to set aside from your side hustle profit.</p>

[[tool:tax-estimator]]

<h3 id="remote-work">6. Remote work settled into hybrid</h3>
<p>Many employers pulled people back to the office part-time, which squeezed some side hustle hours but also brought back commuting dead time that phone-based hustles fill. If that is you, see our list of <a href="/side-hustles-you-can-start-with-just-your-smartphone/">side hustles you can do from your phone</a>.</p>

<h3 id="local-services">7. Local and hands-on hustles got more valuable</h3>
<p>Because AI cannot mow a lawn, clean a rental or assemble a crib, local services have held their value better than many online tasks. Pressure washing, handyman work, pet care and short-term rental turnovers remain some of the best-paying side hustles per hour. Local guides like our <a href="/side-job-hustles-in-texas-emerging-trends-and-opportunities-for-all/">Texas side hustle guide</a> show how much these vary by city.</p>

[[gear:book-side-hustle,book-100-startup,planner|Books and tools for planning a side hustle]]

<h2 id="held-up-vs-faded">What held up and what faded</h2>
<p>Looking back at the side hustles people were excited about in 2023, the pattern is clear: hustles tied to physical work, real expertise or trust held up, while hustles built on cheap, easy-to-copy online output lost value.</p>
<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Held up or grew</th><th>Why</th><th>Faded or got harder</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Delivery and rideshare</td><td>Steady demand, faster payouts</td><td>Basic content writing</td><td>AI drafts pushed prices down</td></tr>
<tr><td>Home and local services</td><td>Can't be automated</td><td>Generic print-on-demand</td><td>Saturated marketplaces</td></tr>
<tr><td>Specialized freelancing</td><td>Clients pay for judgment</td><td>Survey-only income</td><td>Always low pay, more competition</td></tr>
<tr><td>UGC and short-form video</td><td>Brands moved ad budgets to creators</td><td>Faceless AI channels</td><td>Platforms demonetize repetitive content</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reselling</td><td>Thrift and secondhand demand stayed strong</td><td>Dropshipping cheap imports</td><td>Higher ad costs, tariffs and shipping times</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>None of the "faded" categories is dead. They simply reward people who bring a real edge, such as a niche audience, a distinctive style or genuine expertise, rather than anyone with a free account.</p>

<h2 id="whats-next">Where side hustles are headed</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Specialists win.</strong> The gap between generic and specialized freelancers keeps widening. "Email marketing for Shopify stores" beats "freelance writer." Our <a href="/digital-marketing-side-hustle/">digital marketing side hustle guide</a> shows how to niche down.</li>
  <li><strong>Hybrid stacks.</strong> More people combine one flexible gig app for quick cash with one skill-based hustle that grows over time.</li>
  <li><strong>Trust and reputation matter more.</strong> As AI content floods platforms, reviews, referrals and a visible track record become the main way clients choose who to hire.</li>
  <li><strong>Rules keep moving.</strong> Worker classification, platform fees and tax rules will keep changing. Keep records and revisit your numbers every year.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="before-you-start">Five questions to ask before you start</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>How many hours can I really give this each week?</strong> Be honest about family, commute and energy. Pick a hustle that pays off within that window.</li>
  <li><strong>How fast do I need the money?</strong> If you need cash this month, gig apps and selling what you own beat freelancing or content creation, which take months to build.</li>
  <li><strong>What does it cost to start, and what does it cost to keep going?</strong> Count tools, subscriptions, platform fees, gas and insurance, not just the upfront price.</li>
  <li><strong>Does my employer allow it?</strong> Read your employment agreement for non-compete, conflict-of-interest or moonlighting clauses.</li>
  <li><strong>Could this grow if it works?</strong> Some hustles stay capped by your hours. Others, such as freelancing, products and courses, can turn into a real business.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="how-to-start">How to start a side hustle in 2026</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Decide what you want from it:</strong> fast cash, a new skill, or a future business. That choice points to gig work, freelancing or products.</li>
  <li><strong>Take inventory of your time and skills.</strong> Five steady hours a week beats twenty hours you won't actually work.</li>
  <li><strong>Start with one hustle and give it 90 days</strong> before judging it.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> Open a dedicated checking account and set aside 25–30% of profit for taxes. A book like [[amazon:book-psych-money|The Psychology of Money]] helps you decide what to do with the rest.</li>
  <li><strong>Track everything:</strong> mileage, supplies, software and the business share of your phone and internet.</li>
  <li><strong>Review every quarter.</strong> Drop what doesn't pay, raise prices on what does.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
<p>Side hustles in 2026 are easier to start and harder to coast on than they were in 2023. The tools are better, payouts are faster and tax reporting is simpler for most small sellers, but competition for easy online work is fiercer. Pick a hustle that uses something AI can't replace, whether that is a physical service, deep expertise or a local reputation. If you want help choosing, the <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a> from Better Side Hustles matches you with ideas based on your time, budget and skills.</p>
